.TH CMP 1
.SH NAME
cmp \- compare two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmp
[
.B \-l
] [
.B \-s
]
file1 file2 [ skip1 [ skip2 ] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The two files are
compared.
(If
.I file1
is `\-',
the standard input is used.)
Under default options,
.I cmp
makes no comment if the files are the same;
if they differ, it announces the byte and line number
at which the difference occurred.
If one file is an initial subsequence
of the other, that fact is noted.
.PP
Options:
.TP 6
.B  \-l
Print the byte number (decimal) and the
differing bytes (octal) for each difference.
.TP 6
.B  \-s
Print nothing for differing files;
return codes only.
It suppresses the
.B "cannot open"
diagnostic as well.
.SH "SKIPPING A PREFIX"
.B Note:
.I cmp
accepts two further operands after the file names, and v7's page did not mention either.
.I Skip1
and
.I skip2
are counts of bytes to be read and discarded from
.I file1
and
.I file2
respectively before the comparison starts.
Both files are read forward to get there, so either may be a pipe.
.PP
The byte and line numbers reported afterwards count from the
.B first compared byte,
not from the start of the file.
.PP
A count beginning with
.B 0
is octal, and any other is decimal.
.B Note:
v7 selected base 8 on the leading zero and then accepted the digits
.B 8
and
.B 9
regardless, so
.B 018
meant sixteen \- an octal number with a digit octal has not got.
The parse stops at the first digit the base does not have, as every other number in C does,
so
.B 018
now means one.
.PP
Reaching end of file during a skip is reported as
.B "EOF on"
that file, with exit code 1.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit code 0 is returned for identical
files, 1 for different files, and 2 for an
inaccessible or missing argument.
.PP
.B Note:
every diagnostic goes to the
.B standard output,
not the diagnostic output.
That is v7's and is left alone.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
diff(1), comm(1), sum(1)
